  body
    script(src='https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.2.1/jquery.min.js')
    script(src="https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/3.3.7/js/bootstrap.min.js" integrity="sha384-Tc5IQib027qvyjSMfHjOMaLkfuWVxZxUPnCJA7l2mCWNIpG9mGCD8wGNIcPD7Txa" crossorigin="anonymous")
    script(src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/reveal.js/5.2.1/plugin/highlight/highlight.min.js")
    .reveal
      // Any section element inside of this container is displayed as a slide
      .slides
        section
          h1 Challenge Overview
          p
            | Create a Satellite Telemetry Dashboard. 
          h3 Goal:
          p
            | Display and manage telemetry data from satellites using a defined API and React FrontEnd.
            ul 
              li 
                a(href='#/backend') Backend
              li 
                a(href='#/frontend') Frontend
              li 
                a(href='#/packaging') Packaging and deployments
              li
                a(href='#/improvements') Potential improvements
        section#backend
          section
            h2 Backend
            h3 Objective
            p 
              | Design and implement a REST API to manage satellite telemetry data.
            ul
              li Flask with SQLite storage backend.
              li Vite is used to run both the back and front end and control the communication between them: 
              pre 
                code(data-trim data-noescape data-line-numbers) 
                  | export default defineConfig({
                  |   plugins: [react()],
                  |   server: {
                  |     proxy: {
                  |       '/telemetry': 'http://localhost:5000/',
                  |     },
                  |   }
                  | })
              li The API revolves around the 
                code /telemetry 
                a(href='#/api') endpoint.
          section#api
            h2 API
            ul
              li POST 
                code /telemetry: 
                | Add a new telemetry entry.
              ul 
                li Request body should include:
                pre
                  code(data-trim data-noescape data-line-numbers)
                    | {"satelliteId": "string", 
                    |  "timestamp": "ISO 8601 datetime", 
                    |  "altitude": "number", 
                    |  "velocity": "number", 
                    |  "status": "string"}
              li GET 
                code /telemetry: 
                | Retrieve all telemetry data.
              ul
                li Optional query parameters:
                ul
                  li 
                    code satelliteId 
                    | Filter by satellite ID.
                  li 
                    code status 
                    | Filter by health status (e.g., “healthy”, “critical”).
              li GET 
                code /telemetry/:id 
                | Retrieve a specific telemetry entry by ID.
              li DELETE 
              code /telemetry/:id 
              | Delete a specific telemetry entry.
          section 
            h2 Implementation
            ul
              li Flask based backend. 
              ul 
              li Sources
                ul
                  li I leaned heavily on the 
                    a(href="https://blog.miguelgrinberg.com/post/the-flask-mega-tutorial-part-i-hello-world") FlaskMega Tutorial 
                    | for the backend structure and ideas.
                  li And the 
                    a(href="https://blog.miguelgrinberg.com/post/create-a-react-flask-project-in-2025") React + Flask series 
                    | help with getting react and Flask to talk to each other.
              li Kee points
                ul
                  li Flask framework API functions are handled in api.py (this could be broken out for greater Modularity).
                  li Separate function to do validations for easier testing.
                  li Utilities and configuration items are in util.py.
                  li SQLite database used for a data store.
                  li Pagination implemented with sorting (defaulting to ascending id).
        section#frontend
          section
            h2 Frontend
            h3 Objective
            p 
              | Design and implement a web interface to display and manage satellite telemetry data.
            p Expected functionality:
              ul
                li Display a table of telemetry data with columns: Satellite ID, Timestamp, Altitude, Velocity, Health Status.
                li Allow users to filter telemetry data by Satellite ID and Health Status.
                li Provide a form to add new telemetry entries.
                li Allow users to delete telemetry entries stored in the backend.
          section 
            h2 Implementation
            ul 
              li Utilizes React
              ul 
                li Utilizes React's useState, useEffect, and useRef
                ul 
                  li useState for keeping track of items in the UI such as telemetry info, sorting states, pagination, and data for graphing.
                  li useEffect is used for fetching and filtering of data.
                  li useRef is only currently used to initialize an empty temetry graph.
                li Most of the code is centered around app.jsx which functions as the controller for all frontend functionality.
                li Uses formData to manage user inputs to be sent the backend.
          section 
            h2 Design
            ul 
              li Intended to be more of a dashboard feel.
              li Only a single page layout was implemented for this to meet requirements. This should be broken out in the future to allow users to go deeper into the data.
              li Colors are picked with the goal to be somewhat contrasting to increase accassability.
              li Errors with validation or backend communication are reported to the user so the user knows what went wrong.
          section 
            h2 Working With the Backend
            ul 
              li Input validation happens on the client side before being sent to the backend for processing.
              ul 
                li This includes ensuring that a record is complete, date formatting, and numbers are valid (like velocity which cannot be negative).
              li Async calls and promises to the backend are used to not block while the user is waiting.
        section#packaging
          section
            h2 Packaging and Deployments
            ul 
              li Local Development
              ul
                li Local developemnt requires Python 3.11+ for backend and node, NPM, and Yarn for frontend. See README for instructions.
              li Docker
              ul
                li A pre-built Docker image is pushed to Docker Hub to allow for an easy spin up.
                li It contains both backend and frontend implimentations
              li Helm & k8s
              ul 
                li Helm charts are provided to allow fo easy deployment into a Kuberentes based system.
        section#improvements
          section
            h2 Potential Improvements
            ul
              li Break apart front end and backend.
              li Break out the data store.
              li Backend improvements.
              ul
                li Add more configuration options.
                li Add other health types.
                li Add Authentication.
              li Frontend Improvements
              ul
                li UI improvements (color scheme, buttons, add pages, etc).
                li Break up App.jsx to be more modular.
                li Add ability to stream data.
